==================================================
OUTLIER ENGINE
==================================================

Create a dedicated analytics engine:

lib/analytics/outlier/

Implement:

calculateOutlierScore()
calculateExpectedViews()
calculateChannelBaseline()
calculateAgeAdjustedPerformance()

Core concept:

Outlier Ratio =
actual views / expected views

The expected views should be based on the channel's historical performance at a comparable age.

For example:

A video that is 48 hours old should primarily be compared with previous videos from that channel around 48 hours old.

Use robust statistics.

Prefer median over mean when appropriate because viral videos can heavily distort averages.

Consider:

- channel median
- channel percentile distribution
- video age
- format
- Shorts vs long-form
- upload frequency
- channel size
- niche
- historical performance

Do not allow one viral video to completely distort the baseline.

Create normalized scores.

Example:

1x = normal
2x = above normal
5x = strong outlier
10x = major breakout
20x+ = extreme breakout

Also calculate:

outlierPercentile

For example:

Top 1%
Top 5%
Top 10%

==================================================
SHORTS VS LONG-FORM
==================================================

Shorts and long-form must NEVER share the same baseline.

Create separate analytics models.

For Shorts analyze:

- views
- views/hour
- views/day
- outlier ratio
- engagement
- channel baseline
- upload frequency
- duration
- title patterns

For long-form analyze:

- views
- views/hour
- views/day
- outlier ratio
- engagement
- duration
- channel baseline
- upload frequency
- title patterns

Create:

format = SHORT / LONG / LIVE

==================================================
VIEWS PER HOUR
==================================================

Implement:

VPH = views / hoursSincePublished

But also implement:

Relative VPH =
video VPH / channel median VPH at comparable age

This should be more useful than raw VPH.

Display both.

==================================================
ENGAGEMENT
==================================================

Calculate:

likeRate =
likes / views

commentRate =
comments / views

engagementRate =
(likes + comments) / views

Handle missing statistics safely.

Never divide by zero.

